      "math_academy_id": "q-75270",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5{e}^{3t},\\,y=6{t}^{2}-9,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical.",
      "worked_solution": "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=15{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=15{e}^{3\\cdot 0}=15\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$",
